WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.100
- You know, products
can sometimes get built

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.910
in a matter of months,

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:09.680
but culture is built day by
day, over years and decades.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:12.010
And I think it's really important

00:00:12.010 --> 00:00:14.300
to take a long-term approach

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:17.880
to how we think about remote work.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.020
- [Narrator] Mark Zuckerberg
here is discussing a problem

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:22.740
on the minds of many business leaders.

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.350
Research shows that when
employees leave places

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.210
with a high concentration
of specialized workers,

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:30.050
innovation is stifled.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:31.460
And as the pandemic continues,

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:32.970
more companies are letting workers

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:36.320
move far away from the
office, sometimes permanently.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:38.410
So why is proximity important,

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:40.200
and what are companies doing to adapt

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.113
to their decentralized workforces?

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:44.880
Over the decades,

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.480
tech companies like
Apple, Tesla and Facebook

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.340
opened offices in Silicon Valley,

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:52.290
creating a hub of
specialized tech workers.

00:00:52.290 --> 00:00:55.070
Business leaders and economists
say that this proximity

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:57.383
is key to the success of a new venture.

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:00.990
Research from the labor
economist, Enrico Moretti

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:02.770
suggests that when specialized workers

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:03.870
live near one another,

00:01:03.870 --> 00:01:06.700
their companies gain a
competitive advantage.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.938
- Places like Silicon
Valley are very expensive

00:01:09.938 --> 00:01:13.760
because labor costs more,
office space costs more,

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.670
but they also come with
a lot of advantages,

00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.850
and especially if you are
in the innovation sector.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:21.510
There's a growing body of research

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:22.950
that points to the fact that

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:26.670
firms and inventors and innovators

00:01:26.670 --> 00:01:29.330
are particularly productive and creative

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.090
in places like Silicon Valley.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:32.110
- [Narrator] In a recent paper,

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:34.010
Moretti traced the migration patterns

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:37.540
in patent filings of more
than 900,000 scientists,

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.670
inventors and engineers.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.040
He found that when a worker leaves

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:42.740
an industry specialized city,

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:45.767
that worker innovates at a slower pace.

00:01:45.767 --> 00:01:48.467
- In my data, I'm following
the same person over time.

00:01:49.466 --> 00:01:53.580
I'm seeing an innovator
or inventor in rural Ohio,

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.160
in Cleveland, you know,

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.290
in this small place, in the small cluster,

00:01:57.290 --> 00:01:59.070
and I see how many patents they create.

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.500
And then I see that person
moving to Silicon Valley

00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:03.550
or Seattle or Boston.

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.990
And what you see is that the same person

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:10.260
becomes immediately
significantly more productive.

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:11.770
- [Narrator] Moretti says that on average,

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.470
a worker in a city with many
companies in their field

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:17.510
will produce better
ideas at a faster pace.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:18.670
But during the pandemic,

00:02:18.670 --> 00:02:21.380
people have left tech
clusters like San Francisco,

00:02:21.380 --> 00:02:22.623
some permanently.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.390
Companies say that some employees

00:02:25.390 --> 00:02:28.190
want to be closer to their
home towns and families,

00:02:28.190 --> 00:02:30.400
others are looking to save money.

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.730
Though the median price
for a one bedroom rental

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:34.040
fell in 2020,

00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:37.200
San Francisco remains one of
the most expensive Metro areas

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:38.790
in the US.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:39.830
Over recent years,

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:41.700
that led to a migration from the Bay

00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:46.190
to places like Sacramento,
Las Vegas and Austin, Texas.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:47.630
Companies have had to re-imagine

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:48.770
how they'll keep innovating

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:50.593
while their workforces spread out.

00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:55.000
- When you try to measure
where innovation comes from,

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:56.170
it's hard to force it.

00:02:56.170 --> 00:02:58.390
It's hard to tell people
we're gonna lock you in a room

00:02:58.390 --> 00:02:59.980
and you're gonna be innovators.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:00.813
- [Narrator] For a decade,

00:03:00.813 --> 00:03:04.090
Laszlo Bock led people
operations at Google.

00:03:04.090 --> 00:03:07.720
In 2017 he launched his own company, Hulu.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.870
Hulu develops products that
influence worker behavior.

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:13.820
It's headquartered near Alphabet's
campus in Mountain View.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.690
- In my prior job when I was at Alphabet,

00:03:15.690 --> 00:03:17.990
we talked a lot about what
we called casual collisions

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:19.640
or moments of serendipity,

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:21.860
where people casually
bump into one another.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:23.900
So famously Google News,

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:27.230
which is an aggregate of
websites evolved because

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.090
following the events of September 11th,

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:31.740
there were two engineers
standing in line at a cafe,

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:32.960
one of them was complaining about

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:34.530
how it's hard to keep up with all the news

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:36.900
and the other said I could
pull something together.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.980
- [Narrator] Bock says that
these moments of serendipity

00:03:38.980 --> 00:03:40.970
are the building blocks of innovation,

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.400
but they come most easily in person.

00:03:43.400 --> 00:03:45.388
- Bringing people physically together,

00:03:45.388 --> 00:03:48.380
and more importantly,
emotionally together is vital,

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.440
a prerequisite for innovation.

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.510
- [Narrator] Bock says that
innovation in the workplace

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:54.810
comes from low affinity distance.

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:56.460
- Affinity distance is central

00:03:56.460 --> 00:03:57.750
to innovation and productivity.

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:00.340
It's that sense of
closeness, of connectivity,

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.780
of psychological safety,

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:03.130
that allows somebody to say,

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:05.913
I've got a crazy idea, I'm gonna try that.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:08.240
And the challenge with remote work

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.240
is when you've been around people

00:04:10.240 --> 00:04:11.960
and hung out with them and
talked about their kids

00:04:11.960 --> 00:04:14.410
and had water cooler conversations,

00:04:14.410 --> 00:04:16.690
you get low affinity distance,

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:18.760
we get closer to one another.

00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:19.750
- [Narrator] Some companies prefer

00:04:19.750 --> 00:04:21.940
to develop those bonds in person

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.720
and others are leaning
into the remote experience.

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.940
- There's a handful of
companies that are saying,

00:04:26.940 --> 00:04:28.370
we're just gonna be remote forever,

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:30.240
we're gonna hire people
wherever they wanna be,

00:04:30.240 --> 00:04:31.800
and that's how it's gonna be.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:32.900
- [Narrator] In the short term,

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:34.870
companies are turning
to their own products

00:04:34.870 --> 00:04:36.560
to build community.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.520
Facebook, for example, is adding
workplace friendly features

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.080
to its portal video conferencing product.

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:44.440
- So we use our own
product, our nudge engine,

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.550
and it's great at finding
those little moments

00:04:46.550 --> 00:04:50.730
where people can actually connect
and be human and innovate.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:51.840
- [Narrator] Humu's nudge product

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.560
sends messages to
employees at specific times

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.610
to encourage behaviors
that boost productivity.

00:04:57.610 --> 00:05:00.470
For example, it might
send a nudge that suggests

00:05:00.470 --> 00:05:04.060
changing up weekly virtual
team bonding activities.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:08.040
- By changing the prompt or
the type of event every month

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.590
or two or three months,

00:05:09.590 --> 00:05:10.940
you refresh the experience

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:13.030
and you help bring people together.

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:14.410
- [Narrator] Other
companies are cutting down

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:16.800
on avoidable meetings
and designing workflows

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:19.860
that accommodate people who
live in different time zones.

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:21.270
- An example of this might be

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:24.220
having a really, really
clear problem statement

00:05:24.220 --> 00:05:28.220
in a Google document and a
proposed solution to that,

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:31.030
and having multiple people
being able to weigh in

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:34.230
on that document, giving
comments on their own time,

00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:36.080
and then that being sufficient enough

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.430
for the project leader to
synthesize all that information

00:05:40.430 --> 00:05:44.030
and make the next iteration
of that proposed solution.

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:45.090
- [Narrator] Dominique Baillet

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:47.780
shapes the employee
experience at Coinbase,

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.950
a San Francisco tech company
that is now remote first.

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:52.410
She says, it's an opportunity

00:05:52.410 --> 00:05:54.700
to make the workplace
more efficient for people

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:57.101
who may have otherwise been marginalized.

00:05:57.101 --> 00:05:58.430
- Even before work environments

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:00.500
had been remote friendly before,

00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:03.210
remote first creates more
equitable playing field

00:06:03.210 --> 00:06:05.580
to root out some of the
systemic disadvantages

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:07.110
of not being in the office

00:06:07.110 --> 00:06:11.010
when the majority of your
colleagues are in the office.

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:12.550
- [Narrator] At other
companies like Facebook,

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.670
corporate offsite gatherings
could invert into onsite

00:06:15.670 --> 00:06:17.840
for remote workers at headquarters,

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.260
but alphabet, for example,

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:22.900
plans to have workers return
to the office when it's safe.

00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:25.880
Surveys conducted by economists
say that after the pandemic,

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:30.080
about 25% of all full workdays
will be performed from home.

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:31.040
In the meantime,

00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:33.640
companies will keep revamping
the remote experience.

